## Summary

Implements the design proposed in [this comment](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/55247#issuecomment-5800647222) on #55247 ("Use smartCase for Find").

The global **Search** panel already supports `search.smartCase`: when enabled, a search is case-insensitive unless the query itself contains an uppercase letter, in which case it becomes case-sensitive automatically. The in-editor **Find widget** (`Ctrl+F` / `Cmd+F`) had no equivalent, forcing users to manually toggle "Match Case" every time. This PR adds a matching `editor.find.smartCase` setting (default `false`, opt-in) for the Find widget.

## Design

Reuses the existing `strings.containsUppercaseCharacter` helper and its `ignoreEscapedChars` handling for regex patterns — the exact same building block `queryBuilder.ts` already relies on for `search.smartCase` — so both features share identical, already-battle-tested semantics instead of a second implementation.

The change is scoped to a single new private method in `FindModelBoundToEditorModel` (`src/vs/editor/contrib/find/browser/findModel.ts`):

```ts
private _getEffectiveMatchCase(): boolean {
	if (this._state.matchCase) {
		return true;
	}
	if (!this._editor.getOption(EditorOption.find).smartCase) {
		return false;
	}
	return containsUppercaseCharacter(this._state.searchString, this._state.isRegex);
}
```

The 6 call sites in that class that previously read `this._state.matchCase` directly (in `_prevSearchPosition`, `_nextSearchPosition`, `_findMatches`, and `_largeReplaceAll`) now call `_getEffectiveMatchCase()` instead. Everything else is intentionally untouched:

- `FindReplaceState.matchCase` and the "Match Case" checkbox in `findWidget.ts` still reflect exactly what the user toggled.
- `preserveCase` / replace behavior is unaffected.
- The one-shot `matchCaseOverride` mechanism used by `editor.action.findWithArgs` is unaffected.

So smart case only changes what *effective* case-sensitivity gets used internally for a search — it never changes what the toggle displays or what gets persisted to find history.

## Test plan

Added a `editor.find.smartCase` suite to `findModel.test.ts` covering:
- [x] Disabled (default) — behavior unchanged for a mixed-case search string.
- [x] Enabled + all-lowercase search string — case-insensitive.
- [x] Enabled + search string containing an uppercase letter — case-sensitive.
- [x] Enabled + `matchCase` explicitly toggled on — stays case-sensitive regardless of the pattern's own casing (explicit toggle isn't overridden).
- [x] Enabled + regex mode with an uppercase letter inside an escape sequence — not treated as case-sensitive, matching `search.smartCase`'s existing `ignoreEscapedChars` behavior.
- [x] CI unit tests

## Summary

- Adds `registerLanguageModelRequestMiddleware` to the Copilot Chat extension API (`getAPI(2)`), so an extension can attach headers to each BYOK language model request: a short-lived credential, the signed-in user, the chat session, the work item.
- Applies to every OpenAI-compatible BYOK provider (`openai`, `azure` including Entra auth, `ollama`, `openrouter`, `xai`, `customoai`, `customendpoint`); Anthropic and Gemini are out of scope.
- Middleware may set what `requestHeaders` may set for that provider, through the same sanitiser. On `customendpoint` that includes `Authorization` and `api-key`, which replace the configured credential; other providers keep their own.
- Middleware can be scoped to a provider group. The request context carries the resolved request URL and the name of the provider group the model was configured in, and the selector accepts `providerGroups`. To make the group name available, the proposed `chatProvider` API now passes it to `provideLanguageModelChatInformation` as `PrepareLanguageModelChatModelOptions.group` (one d.ts field, one extension host line). That part needs API owner sign-off and can be reshaped independently of the rest.

Related: #316794, #329469

## Why an API and not a config value

Organisations that route BYOK models through a gateway (LiteLLM, Portkey, APIM, an internal proxy) need two things per request that `chatLanguageModels.json` cannot provide. The first is a credential that is not a static key: a bearer token from SSO or `vscode.authentication` that rotates and belongs to the user, or a per-provider secret an internal extension manages so nobody pastes keys into settings. The second is context for attribution and cost tagging: which user, which chat session, which ticket. Template variables (#329469) could cover a workspace name or a session id, but not a token from an auth provider or the ticket a developer selected a minute ago. With this API the organisation ships one small extension that knows those values; users only configure the model.

```ts
const copilot = vscode.extensions.getExtension('github.copilot-chat')!.exports.getAPI(2);
copilot.registerLanguageModelRequestMiddleware({
	selector: { vendors: ['customendpoint'] },
	provideRequestHeaders: async ({ modelId }) => {
		const session = await vscode.authentication.getSession('my-sso', ['llm-gateway'], { silent: true });
		return {
			Authorization: `Bearer ${session?.accessToken}`,
			'x-litellm-end-user': session?.account.id ?? 'anonymous',
			'x-litellm-tags': `model:${modelId},ticket:${tracker.activeTicket()}`,
		};
	},
});
```

## Why scope to a provider group

One vendor such as `customendpoint` can back several user-configured provider groups: different gateways, or the same gateway with different keys that unlock different model lists. Model ids are not unique across groups. A middleware registered for a vendor alone would send its credential to every group of that vendor, and without the group name in the context the author cannot tell them apart. With `providerGroups` an extension registers once per group. The group name is chosen by the user and can be renamed or reused for a different service, so it is routing, not the security check: the docs require middleware that returns a credential to also verify `context.url`.

```ts
for (const group of ['Acme Premium', 'Acme Budget']) {
	copilot.registerLanguageModelRequestMiddleware({
		selector: { vendors: ['customendpoint'], providerGroups: [group] },
		provideRequestHeaders: async ({ url }) => {
			if (new URL(url).origin !== 'https://gateway.example.com') {
				return {};
			}
			return { Authorization: `Bearer ${await getToken(group)}` };
		},
	});
}
```

<details>
<summary>Technical context for AI-assisted review</summary>

### Intent and previous behavior

`OpenAIEndpoint` read `requestHeaders` from the model configuration once at construction and sanitised them against a private reserved-header list. Nothing outside the configuration file could influence request headers, and header merging was case-sensitive, so `X-Shared` and `x-shared` could both be sent. `CustomEndpointOAIEndpoint` already let configuration override `Authorization` and `api-key` and suppressed its default credential when it did.

Core passed the provider group name to the main thread as `options.group`, but `extHostLanguageModels.ts` dropped it before calling the provider, so an extension could not learn which group a model belongs to.

### Implementation

`byok/common/requestHeaders.ts` holds the reserved names, the `proxy-*`/`sec-*` prefix check and a case-insensitive merge, used by the endpoint.

`byok/common/languageModelRequestMiddleware.ts` is a registry service. `provideRequestHeaders` snapshots the matching registrations, invokes them concurrently, and merges results in registration order. Each invocation is bounded by a 10 s timeout and by the request's cancellation token. A registration disposed while in flight has its result discarded. The registry does not filter header names. The selector matches on `vendors`, `modelIds` and `providerGroups`.

`OpenAIEndpoint.applyRequestHeaders` sanitises middleware headers with the same rules as configuration headers, under a separate 20-header budget and with warnings attributed to the middleware rather than the model configuration. `cloneWithTokenOverride` carries the headers to the clone. `CustomEndpointOAIEndpoint` treats a credential header from middleware like one from configuration and suppresses its default credential. `AbstractOpenAICompatibleLMProvider.applyRequestMiddleware` runs the registry for a request with the endpoint's resolved URL and the model's group name, and is also called from the Azure Entra path, which builds its own endpoint.

`AbstractLanguageModelChatProvider.provideLanguageModelChatInformation` reads `group` from the options and stores it as `providerGroup` on every model it returns. `src/vscode-dts/vscode.proposed.chatProvider.d.ts` declares `PrepareLanguageModelChatModelOptions.group`, and `extHostLanguageModels.ts` forwards it.

`CopilotExtensionApi.version` is now 2.

### Behavior and constraints

- Header names middleware may set are the ones `requestHeaders` may set for the provider. Transport-owned and forbidden headers (`Content-Type`, `X-Request-Id`, `Host`, `Cookie`, `Proxy-*`, `Sec-*`, ...) are dropped and logged at warn level for both sources.
- Middleware headers override same-named configuration headers, compared case-insensitively.
- Middleware values are never `${apiKey}`-interpolated, so the configured key is not exposed to other extensions. Middleware cannot change the request URL.
- The context carries `url` (the fully resolved request URL) and `providerGroup` (the group name, or `undefined` for models requested without a group). A request without a group never matches a selector with `providerGroups`.
- Model ids are not unique across groups; `modelIds` alone cannot separate two groups on the same gateway.
- `errorBehavior: 'continue'` (default) drops a failing or timed-out middleware's headers and logs; `'fail'` fails the request. Cancellation errors always propagate.
- Registrations are logged at info level with their selector; contributed header names at debug level.
- Open question: should registration be restricted to an allow-list of extension ids, or is installed-and-trusted plus logging sufficient?
- Follow-up: #337203 (stacked on this PR) adds the chat session id to the middleware context, so a gateway can group requests per chat.

</details>

## Validation

- Unit tests for the registry (selector matching including provider groups and the no-group case, ordered case-insensitive merge, concurrency, dispose in flight for both headers and errors, continue and fail, timeout, cancellation), for the custom endpoint (middleware overrides configured headers, replaces the credential and suppresses the default one, survives the token-override clone, receives the resolved URL and group name, and keeps a credential scoped to its group when two groups share a URL with different keys) and for the base endpoint (middleware credential dropped).
- Manual: dev build with a throwaway extension registering middleware for `customendpoint` against a local request-capturing OpenAI-compatible endpoint, exercised both through `vscode.lm` and from the chat view. The middleware `Authorization` replaced the configured key on both requests, the context headers arrived, a middleware header overrode the same-named configured header while an unrelated configured header was kept, `Content-Type`, `X-Request-Id` and `Host` from the middleware were dropped with a warning in the Copilot log, and a middleware with a non-matching selector was not invoked. A second manual run configured three `customendpoint` groups at the same URL with different keys, registered one middleware per group for the first two with its own bearer token, and sent one request per model: the endpoint received each group's own middleware token for the first two and the configured key for the third, and each middleware was invoked only for its own group with the resolved URL and group name in the context.
- `vitest` for `src/extension/byok` and `src/extension/api`, `npm run typecheck-client` at the repo root, `eslint` on changed files, and `npm run hygiene` pass.
- The Azure Entra path is covered by a provider-level test that stubs `vscode.authentication.getSession` and checks that middleware scoped to the group receives the resolved Azure URL and the group name, and that its headers reach the endpoint while the Entra token stays the credential.

## Description

The voice mode mic button showed the *action* glyph (open mic to unmute, crossed-out mic to mute) plus a red treatment while muted, which reads inverted against common conventions. The button should instead reflect the microphone's current state.

Changes in `agentsVoiceWidget.ts` and the shared `headerComponent.ts`:

- **Icon reflects state, not action** — muted renders `codicon-mic-off`, active renders `codicon-mic`.
- **Dropped red muted treatment** — mute button uses `descriptionForeground` in both states (hover behavior unchanged).
- **Left untouched** — aria-labels, titles, and `aria-pressed`, which correctly describe the toggle action.

```ts
// before: glyph described the action
muteBtn.classList.toggle('codicon-mic', props.isMuted);
muteBtn.classList.toggle('codicon-mic-off', !props.isMuted);
const muteColor = props.isMuted ? 'var(--vscode-editorError-foreground)' : 'var(--vscode-descriptionForeground)';

// after: glyph reflects current state
muteBtn.classList.toggle('codicon-mic-off', props.isMuted);
muteBtn.classList.toggle('codicon-mic', !props.isMuted);
const muteColor = 'var(--vscode-descriptionForeground)';
```

