### Current behavior

- The icon comes from [`getProviderIconForIdentity()`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/widget/input/modelPicker/modelProviderIcons.ts#L28-L53), which looks for hard-coded strings in the model's vendor, family, id and name.
- Any model from an extension other than Copilot that doesn't set the proposed `isBYOK` is [marked `isBYOK: true`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vs/workbench/api/browser/mainThreadLanguageModels.ts#L110-L115), and BYOK models [skip the string matching and get the sparkle](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/widget/input/modelPicker/modelProviderIcons.ts#L60-L62). Grok/xAI is the only exception.
- The only override is the proposed [`statusIcon`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vscode-dts/vscode.proposed.chatProvider.d.ts#L79), which [takes precedence over the provider icon](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/widget/input/modelPicker/modelProviderIcons.ts#L71-L73). It's meant for model state (Copilot uses it for warnings), though, not identity.

The [tests](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/test/browser/widget/input/modelPicker/modelProviderIcons.test.ts#L33-L69) show that VS Code deliberately avoids guessing a brand for BYOK models (e.g. a BYOK "Claude Sonnet 5" from vendor `anthropic` gets the generic icon). That makes sense when VS Code is the one doing the guessing. This request is for the provider to state its own icon explicitly, just as it already states `name` and `detail`.

### Motivation

In the picker, the icon is how users tell models apart at a glance. Copilot-served models get recognizable icons, while every model from another extension looks identical. That's especially confusing for:

- **Single-vendor providers** (an extension for one company's models), which want their own logo.
- **Gateways and aggregators** (OpenRouter-style, enterprise proxies, self-hosted), which serve models from several upstream vendors through one extension and want per-model icons.
- **Users with several providers installed**, who can't tell which extension a sparkle model comes from without opening its hover.

### Proposed shape

One option is an optional per-model `iconPath` on `LanguageModelChatInformation`, using the existing [`IconPath`](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vscode-dts/vscode.d.ts#L973) type (`Uri | { light: Uri; dark: Uri } | ThemeIcon`):

```ts
export interface LanguageModelChatInformation {
    // ...
    readonly iconPath?: IconPath;
}
```

That would let a provider either:

- **reuse VS Code's built-in provider icons** with a `ThemeIcon`, e.g. `new ThemeIcon('chat-model-provider-claude')`. The [`chat-model-provider-*` icons](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/1f6488722c942d1d7528a7d949769a69e4a557c3/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/widget/input/modelPicker/modelProviderIcons.ts#L14-L22) are what Copilot-served models already show, so a Claude model from any provider would look the same as Copilot's Claude models, or
- **supply its own icon**, as an icon contributed through `contributes.icons` or as image `Uri`s.

Precedence in the picker would be `statusIcon` (when set), then `iconPath`, then VS Code's built-in matching, then the sparkle.

It has to be per model rather than per vendor, because one provider can serve models from different upstream vendors. A vendor-level default (e.g. an `icon` on the `languageModelChatProviders` contribution point) could be added on top, but wouldn't be enough by itself.

