### Root cause

All three are in `src/vs/editor/browser/controller/editContext/native/nativeEditContext.ts`, which is identical in 1.139.1 and on `main` (f720bc375df41e4f29cf25b4820c76e698578eb8).

**1. `_updateEditContext` replaces only the length of its previous write** ([L420-L423](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/f720bc375df41e4f29cf25b4820c76e698578eb8/src/vs/editor/browser/controller/editContext/native/nativeEditContext.ts#L420-L423))

When `textupdate` fires, Blink has already applied the keyboard's edit to `editContext.text`. Monaco types the edit into the model, and `_updateEditContext` then calls `updateText(0, this._previousEditContextText.length, newText)`, which replaces only as many characters as it wrote last time. Every character the keyboard added stays after the new line, so the buffer becomes the line followed by its own reverse. The model and the file stay correct, but a keyboard that reads the surrounding text (Gboard does after a tap or any other caret move) sees that tail, offers it as a suggestion and computes its edits against it. Before #298272 (first released in 1.111) the whole buffer was replaced with `updateText(0, Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, text)`.

**2. The caret delta ignores the copied prefix and suffix** ([L456-L459](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/f720bc375df41e4f29cf25b4820c76e698578eb8/src/vs/editor/browser/controller/editContext/native/nativeEditContext.ts#L456-L459))

For a collapsed selection `positionDelta = e.selectionStart - (e.updateRangeStart + e.text.length)`. The text passed to `compositionType` is `text`, which also contains the buffer text between the caret and the update range, and it is inserted from `min(caret, updateRangeStart)`; the caret ends at the end of the inserted text plus `positionDelta`. So whenever the update range ends before the caret, the caret lands `caret - updateRangeEnd` characters past the keyboard's selection. Gboard commits a suggestion by first sending a selection-only update, `""` at `[0, 0)` with the selection after the word. In the mid-word case the caret is at 14 and the keyboard's selection at 19: Monaco retypes `[0, 14)` and moves its caret to 14 + 19 = 33, clamped to 24, the end of the line, and pushes that selection to the EditContext. The keyboard's next edit deletes the 8 characters before its caret, now 24, so `ie delta` is removed instead of `charlie `. The delta should be measured from the end of what is typed, `e.selectionStart - (Math.min(selectionStartOffset, e.updateRangeStart) + text.length)`, which equals the current formula whenever the range reaches the caret.

In the first sequence both apply: the keyboard offers the buffer's word, and its delete, taken from the caret that (2) moved to the end of the line, removes all of `kiwi` before it commits `wiiwik `. In the third, the keyboard reads `hereereh` because the buffer continues past the end of the line, and computes its delete and commit against that text.

**3. The prefix and suffix are read at pre-edit offsets from the edited buffer** ([L448-L455](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/f720bc375df41e4f29cf25b4820c76e698578eb8/src/vs/editor/browser/controller/editContext/native/nativeEditContext.ts#L448-L455))

`selectionStartOffset`, `selectionEndOffset` and the update range are offsets into the text before the edit, but the copies read `this._editContext.text`, which already contains the edit whenever Blink applied it before dispatching, as it had in every event recorded with the keyboard above. If the edit changes the length, the copied text is shifted. For example, with the line `dont ` and the caret after the space, an IME that replaces `[0, 4)` with `don't` (autocorrect of the previous word) makes Monaco type `"don't" + editContext.text.substring(4, 5)`, which is `t` after the edit, so the line becomes `don'tt` instead of `don't `. Reading from `_previousEditContextText`, the text last written, which is what those offsets describe, fixes it; with (1) applied, that field equals the buffer as it was before the keyboard's edit. The keyboard above never produced such an edit, so this was reproduced on vscode.dev with a synthetic event that does what Blink does:

```js
// Untitled file whose only line is "dont ", caret at the end.
const ec = document.querySelector('.native-edit-context').editContext;
ec.updateText(0, 4, "don't"); // Blink applies the edit before it fires textupdate
ec.updateSelection(6, 6);
ec.dispatchEvent(new TextUpdateEvent('textupdate', { text: "don't", updateRangeStart: 0, updateRangeEnd: 4, selectionStart: 6, selectionEnd: 6 }));
// The line is now "don'tt".
```

The code is not Android specific. Any `textupdate` that adds characters leaves them in the buffer, so desktop IMEs that read the surrounding text may be affected too; I have not tested desktop.

